WriteArchiveAscii prints every float with `std::to_string` - `%f`, six
decimals, always - where ZenGin printed nine significant digits.

The two writers disagree in both directions and neither is a rounding
nicety. `%f` *pads* every round value, so `0` is written `0.000000` and
`500` is written `500.000000`; and it *truncates* at world magnitudes,
where six decimals is more digits than a float has: ZenGin's
`1511.77087` comes back as `1511.770874`, and anything below 1e-6 - the
retail worlds hold 57 such values in OldCamp alone - collapses to
`0.000000` outright.

The ZenGin form, measured across a retail Gothic II install's ASCII
worlds, is `%.9g` with the three-digit exponent its MSVC CRT always
printed: `range=float:1200`, `=float:10.0000105`,
`trafoOSToWSPos=vec3:1552.21545 756.087585 -6289.54248`,
`bbox3DWS=rawFloat:751.806152 -128.8302 -7089.83691 ...`,
`2.98023224e-008`. Nine significant digits is also exactly what
round-trips a float, so this is byte fidelity only - no parsed value
changes, in either direction, for any input.

Same shape of fix as 0009, which does this for `zCMaterial`'s `texScale`
and `texAniMapDir` strings (eight digits there: a different ZenGin call
site, measured separately). The three text-float writers are covered
here: `write_float`, `write_vec3` and `write_raw_float`, which is what
`write_vec2` and `write_bbox` go through.

After 0045-0047 made the unpacked `zCVob` layout writable, this is the
dominant remaining ASCII defect: OldCamp's 440 struct findings are all
`position`, `bbox`, `speed` and waypoint `direction`.

Upstreamable as-is: a plain fidelity fix in three writer functions, no
API change.

